 Rules that go with the shapes:
 
 - **Abort.** `signal` aborts the stream; the adapter emits `done` with `stop: 'aborted'` and whatever blocks were complete. Unfinished tool calls get synthetic `tool_result`s. The turn ends with `turn_end{reason:'aborted'}`.
-- **Retry.** `rate_limit`, `overloaded`, `network`, `timeout` and `server` retry with exponential backoff, at most 5 attempts, honouring `retryAfterMs`. `auth`, `bad_request`, `context_length` and `quota` (HTTP 402, or a 429 whose message says the plan or balance is spent) never retry. Each try is its own `attempt` event.
+- **Retry.** `rate_limit`, `overloaded`, `network`, `timeout` and `server` retry with exponential backoff, at most 5 attempts, honouring `retryAfterMs`. `auth`, `bad_request`, `context_length` and `quota` (HTTP 402, or a 429 whose message says the plan or balance is spent) never retry. Each try is its own `attempt` event. With `--on-quota wait:<minutes>` (or `onQuota` in user settings), a `quota` error that names when it comes back (a 429 with retry-after) is waited out in the same session, up to that many minutes across the whole run; each wait is a logged attempt and a stderr line, and ends on abort. A spent balance names no time and falls back at once. The default is `fallback`.
 - **Request event.** Carries model, effort, `toolsHash`, `systemHash`, `bodyHash`, TTL and header names with value hashes. Never header values.
 - **Role fallback** starts a new session on the fallback model; it never swaps the model inside a session (cache rule 5).
 - **Quota fallback.** A run that ends on a `quota` error continues on its fallback model (`--fallback`, then `fallback` in `~/.cedia/settings.json`, then the built-in `deepseek` → `anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5`; `none` turns it off; a repo cannot set it). The new session has `parentSession`, a logged reminder that the tree may hold part of the work, what is left of the turn and budget limits, and the first session's changed files as writes the done gate must still see verified. The result adds up both sessions' spend and turns.
+- **Refused history.** A `bad_request` or `context_length` after the session's first accepted request means the provider refuses the history itself (a thinking signature, a tool call it cannot read back, a context past the window), and resending fails the same way. The run goes on once in a new session on the same model, as quota fallback does (`parentSession`, inherited changes, `recall`, a logged reminder); the thread records it as `why: 'rejected'`. A resumed thread whose last session was refused starts a new session. A refused first request is a bad request, not a bad history, and ends the run.
+- **Malformed turns.** A reply with only thinking, or nothing, gets one reminder, then fails the run (an earlier turn's text never stands in as the result). A refusal fails the run. A tool result's text is always a string (an object is serialized; no output is a failed result), and a non-Error throw is named. Calls in one reply that share an id run once and get one result. An unknown tool's name is cut to 64 characters in its result.
 - **Mid-conversation system prompt.** Appended as a system message when `midConversationSystem` is true, otherwise as a user text block.
 - **Parallel tools.** Calls whose `ToolSpec.effect` is `read` or `network` run in a bounded pool (8 at a time); `write_workspace` and `exec` run alone, as ordering barriers. `tool_intent` events are written in assistant block order; results go back as one batch (Anthropic requires all results of a turn in one user message).
 - **Permission order in the log.** `assistant → permission → tool_intent (only if allowed) → tool_result`. A denied call gets `tool_result{status:'denied'}` and no `tool_intent`.
